WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.741
Pushing WireGuard on

00:00:01.553 --> 00:00:02.908
macOS to 5

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:03.970
Gbit / s

00:00:04.494 --> 00:00:05.944
Firezone explains how

00:00:05.756 --> 00:00:06.333
it took its

00:00:06.145 --> 00:00:07.902
macOS WireGuard client

00:00:07.713 --> 00:00:08.562
from about 1

00:00:08.374 --> 00:00:09.635
Gbit / s

00:00:09.447 --> 00:00:10.437
to over 5

00:00:10.249 --> 00:00:11.522
Gbit / s

00:00:11.664 --> 00:00:12.607
by digging through

00:00:12.478 --> 00:00:13.904
XNU's TUN and

00:00:13.716 --> 00:00:14.635
UDP code

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:16.522
including batched syscalls

00:00:16.334 --> 00:00:17.866
and handling backpressure

00:00:18.382 --> 00:00:20.127
Firezone's macOS and

00:00:19.998 --> 00:00:21.400
iOS clients topped

00:00:21.212 --> 00:00:22.120
out around 1

00:00:21.932 --> 00:00:23.075
Gbit / s

00:00:22.887 --> 00:00:23.381
down

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:24.773
with uploads often

00:00:24.585 --> 00:00:25.516
a few hundred

00:00:25.328 --> 00:00:26.624
Mbit / s

00:00:26.684 --> 00:00:27.544
and got worse

00:00:27.356 --> 00:00:28.523
with higher latency

00:00:28.665 --> 00:00:29.325
a sign of

00:00:29.137 --> 00:00:30.233
lost packets

00:00:30.758 --> 00:00:31.831
On two M1

00:00:31.642 --> 00:00:32.680
Macs linked by

00:00:32.492 --> 00:00:33.658
Thunderbolt the client

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.592
now reaches 5.87

00:00:35.404 --> 00:00:36.760
Gbit / s

00:00:36.572 --> 00:00:38.552
up and 5.76

00:00:38.364 --> 00:00:39.472
Gbit / s

00:00:39.284 --> 00:00:39.850
down

00:00:40.374 --> 00:00:41.388
Full story on

00:00:41.199 --> 00:00:42.095
the dashboard
